1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,832
How does the card zone know a call really comes from ecom?

2
00:00:02,882 --> 00:00:07,584
Picture a request that simply says, in its header: I am ecom.

3
00:00:07,634 --> 00:00:09,456
Anyone can type that line.

4
00:00:09,506 --> 00:00:14,208
A name you write yourself is like a name badge made at home: it proves nothing.

5
00:00:14,258 --> 00:00:17,184
Now picture the staff entrance of an office building.

6
00:00:17,234 --> 00:00:21,336
You show an ID card, issued by the building's own pass office.

7
00:00:21,386 --> 00:00:25,656
Reception gives you a day pass, and the pass lists the floors you may visit.

8
00:00:25,706 --> 00:00:28,104
The lift goes only to those floors.

9
00:00:28,154 --> 00:00:34,776
The card zone works the same way for every call: two proofs, from two places, and a list of floors.

10
00:00:34,826 --> 00:00:41,568
The first proof is a certificate: a digital ID card, signed by the card zone's own pass office.

11
00:00:41,618 --> 00:00:44,616
When a caller connects, it shows its certificate.

12
00:00:44,666 --> 00:00:47,352
The card zone shows its own certificate back.

13
00:00:47,402 --> 00:00:49,824
So each side knows who is on the other end.

14
00:00:49,874 --> 00:00:54,000
Because both sides show one, this is called mutual TLS.

15
00:00:54,050 --> 00:01:01,176
If a caller has no certificate from the card zone's pass office, the connection is refused before any request is even read.

16
00:01:01,226 --> 00:01:03,792
The second proof is an access token.

17
00:01:03,842 --> 00:01:06,720
Here, an access token is a day pass.

18
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:11,760
The caller asks for it from the card zone's own login server, using its own secret.

19
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:14,520
The card zone trusts only that login server.

20
00:01:14,570 --> 00:01:18,096
A pass from another product's login server is worth nothing here.

21
00:01:18,146 --> 00:01:21,888
Inside the access token is who it belongs to, and its roles.

22
00:01:21,938 --> 00:01:25,728
A role is a floor you may visit: one kind of operation.

23
00:01:25,778 --> 00:01:28,008
One role lets a caller bind a card.

24
00:01:28,058 --> 00:01:29,880
Another lets it read a card.

25
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:31,584
Another lets it charge a card.

26
00:01:31,634 --> 00:01:36,408
Every operation needs its own role, and anything not listed is refused.

27
00:01:36,458 --> 00:01:39,792
So even the real ecom can only do what its roles allow.

28
00:01:39,842 --> 00:01:44,256
No caller at all holds the role for the card zone's admin operations.

29
00:01:44,306 --> 00:01:46,128
Now watch the product.

30
00:01:46,178 --> 00:01:49,848
These checks have no screen, so watch how the system behaves.

31
00:01:49,898 --> 00:01:52,872
A caller with no certificate knocks on the partner door.

32
00:01:52,922 --> 00:01:54,864
The connection closes at once.

33
00:01:54,914 --> 00:01:57,720
No request is read, and nothing is answered.

34
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:02,520
Next, a caller brings a valid certificate, but a pass from the wrong login server.

35
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:05,256
The card zone answers: unauthorized.

36
00:02:05,306 --> 00:02:09,120
The pass did not come from its own login server, so it counts for nothing.

37
00:02:09,170 --> 00:02:12,024
Now the real ecom calls, with both proofs.

38
00:02:12,074 --> 00:02:14,328
It asks to charge a card by its token.

39
00:02:14,378 --> 00:02:18,360
Its pass carries the role to charge, so the call goes through.

40
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:22,584
Then ecom asks for an admin operation, one that searches cards.

41
00:02:22,634 --> 00:02:24,456
Both proofs pass.

42
00:02:24,506 --> 00:02:29,160
But ecom's pass has no admin role, so the card zone answers: forbidden.

43
00:02:29,210 --> 00:02:32,160
Three refusals, for three different reasons.

44
00:02:32,210 --> 00:02:34,536
No certificate means no connection.

45
00:02:34,586 --> 00:02:36,912
A bad pass means unauthorized.

46
00:02:36,962 --> 00:02:38,904
A missing role means forbidden.

47
00:02:38,954 --> 00:02:43,296
Each answer tells the operator exactly which check stopped the call.

48
00:02:43,346 --> 00:02:47,304
The card zone keeps its own pass office and its own login server.

49
00:02:47,354 --> 00:02:52,368
Neither is shared with ecom, so nothing outside the room can hand out the room's passes.

50
00:02:52,418 --> 00:02:54,240
What does this protect?

51
00:02:54,290 --> 00:02:57,624
Steal only the certificate, and you have no pass.

52
00:02:57,674 --> 00:03:01,128
Steal only the pass, and you cannot even connect.

53
00:03:01,178 --> 00:03:06,912
Steal both from ecom, and you still reach only ecom's floors, never the admin floor.

54
00:03:06,962 --> 00:03:13,704
And a mistake inside ecom cannot give ecom more power, because only the card zone grants the roles.

55
00:03:13,754 --> 00:03:19,488
This protects every card in the room, and the bank's trust that only its chosen callers can touch them.

56
00:03:19,538 --> 00:03:21,336
Back to the staff entrance.

57
00:03:21,386 --> 00:03:26,472
An ID card from the building's own office, a day pass, and only your floors.

58
00:03:26,522 --> 00:03:29,928
Next: how the card zone talks to every card network.
